  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I checked each result with a read-back.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has the time field `timestamp`. Response codes are in `response`, with a `response.keyword` subfield that I used for grouping.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-d6861880 Web logs' has two panels, both built on the data view:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of count by `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Responses by HTTP code\": a bar chart of count by `response.keyword` terms, top 10.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-d6861880 5xx errors' is a `.es-query` rule on `stackAlerts`, checking every 1m. It counts requests where `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\" over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is greater than 10. The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n4. **Panel rename:** The response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\". I re-read the dashboard and the new title is in place. I read the rule back: `enabled: true`, status `ok`.\n\nChoices I made:\n- The 5xx range check uses string comparison on `response.keyword`, because the `response` field is typed as a string in this data view. This works for three-digit codes.\n- The response-code panel uses a bar chart, not a pie.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
